+  // ── mindmap-pass 2026-11-10 (competitor): what-is-investment-management ──
+  {
+    "updated": "2026-11-10",
+    "slug": "what-is-investment-management",
+    "title": "What Is Investment Management and What AUM Means for You",
+    "metaDescription": "Investment management means choosing and monitoring investments for a goal. Learn how DIY compares to hiring an adviser and what AUM means for account fees.",
+    "h1": "What Is Investment Management and How to Choose DIY or an Adviser",
+    "cardBlurb": "Learn what investment management involves, how managing your own portfolio compares to hiring an adviser, and what assets under management means for fees.",
+    "introText": "Investment management is choosing, monitoring, and adjusting investments for a specific financial goal. In the guides we publish here, we show the tradeoffs between managing money yourself and paying an ongoing professional fee. You can manage your own portfolio by making every trade yourself, or you can hire an adviser for ongoing oversight.\n\nAt ModernWallet, we show the math, name what moves it, and explain the tradeoff. Choosing between investing on your own and hiring a professional depends on the services you require, all-in costs, and potential conflicts of interest.",
+    "sections": [
+      {
+        "heading": "What Is Investment Management and Whether to Invest on Your Own",
+        "body": "Investment management involves selecting securities, tracking their performance against defined objectives, and reallocating positions over time. Per guidance from [Investor.gov on investing on your own](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/investing-your-own), the first step in self-directed investing is creating a clear financial plan. That plan establishes how much capital you invest, how long you plan to invest, your specific goals, and your risk tolerance.\n\nWhen you invest on your own, you accept sole responsibility for every investment decision. Investor.gov notes that self-directed investors execute trades through online investing platforms, direct investing programs, or dividend reinvestment plans. \n\nIn contrast, hiring an investment professional delegates ongoing portfolio oversight to an external manager. [Investor.gov on investment advisers](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional/investment-advisers) notes that an investment adviser is a firm or person paid to give advice about securities. Advisers generally provide ongoing recommendations about buying, selling, or holding securities."
+      },
+      {
+        "heading": "Services That Explain What Investment Management Covers",
+        "body": "An investment manager provides ongoing portfolio oversight, performance reviews, and asset selection tailored to client goals. Under guidance published by [Investor.gov on working with an investment professional](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional), investment advisers are required to act in your best interest and cannot place their own interests ahead of yours. \n\nAdvisers may also offer optional financial planning services alongside ongoing portfolio monitoring. In discretionary accounts, an adviser makes trades and delegated decisions. \n\nThe scope of work provided by an adviser differs substantially from transaction-based brokerage services. As detailed on [Investor.gov regarding the relationship summary (Form CRS)](https://www.investor.gov/CRS), brokers are generally transactional professionals paid commissions or markups per transaction. Investment advisers focus on managing your portfolio over time. Dual-registered firms operate under both models, so you should understand when they act as a broker and when as an adviser."
+      },
+      {
+        "heading": "What Assets Under Management Means for Account Fees",
+        "body": "According to the [Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) investor bulletin on Form ADV](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-71), an adviser must disclose the amount of client assets managed in Form ADV Part 2A. \n\nInvestor.gov states that an adviser's fee is typically based on the value of all of the assets held in your account with the adviser. The Form CRS disclosure guidance explains that an asset-based fee can cost more than transaction-based fees. However, an asset-based fee aligns with continuous portfolio monitoring and delegated trading authority.\n\n Form ADV Part 2A requires firms to disclose whether fees are negotiable, how billing occurs, and whether custodial or fund expenses apply. Our [flat-fee vs AUM financial advisers comparison](/compare/flat-fee-vs-aum-based-financial-advisors/) covers the cost math for these pricing models."
+      },
+      {
+        "heading": "Decision Framework for Managing Investments or Hiring an Adviser",
+        "body": "Deciding between self-directed investing and hiring an investment adviser depends on required services, total costs, and potential conflicts. Sourced regulatory guidance highlights six core factors: required services, account limits, total costs, payment structure, potential conflicts, and legal or disciplinary history. Our judgment is that self-directed investing works best when you want complete control and have a clear financial plan.\n\n| Your situation | Do it yourself | Hire an adviser | What to check |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Portfolio planning | Create your own financial plan | Receive ongoing advice and optional planning | Required services and account limits |\n| Trading authority | Retain sole responsibility for trades | Delegate discretionary trades | Client contract terms and trading discretion |\n| Fee structure | Not published | Pay an ongoing asset-based fee on total account value | Fee schedules and deduction methods |\n| Oversight requirements | Monitor and rebalance your own holdings | Rely on continuous professional oversight | Form CRS relationship summary disclosures |\n| Professional history | No external professional evaluation required | Research background via public databases | Form ADV Part 1 and Part 2 disciplinary history |\n\nOur suggested order for evaluating this decision begins by determining whether you need ongoing monitoring. If you only require occasional guidance, paying an ongoing asset-based fee may be unnecessary. If you prefer continuous portfolio adjustments and delegated execution, hiring an adviser provides that service."
+      },
+      {
+        "heading": "Steps to Check an Investment Adviser Before Hiring",
+        "body": "Before hiring an investment manager, examine their regulatory registration and public disciplinary record. The SEC Investor Bulletin confirms that Form ADV is the uniform registration document filed by investment advisers with the SEC or state securities authorities. Form ADV contains three separate sections that provide verifiable background details on advisory firms and their individual representatives.\n\nPart 1 uses a check-the-box format covering ownership, business practices, and disciplinary events involving the firm or its employees. Part 2A is a plain-English narrative brochure answering 18 mandatory items, including advisory services, fee schedules, methods of analysis, and material disciplinary disclosures occurring within the past ten years. Part 2B supplements detail the educational background, five-year business history, and disciplinary events of supervised individuals who formulate investment advice or direct client portfolios.\n\nRetail investors can review Part 3, known as Form CRS, to examine core services, fees, conflicts of interest, and required conduct standards. The SEC makes these brochures and relationship summaries accessible on the [Investment Adviser Public Disclosure (IAPD) database](https://adviserinfo.sec.gov). You can also run background searches through the [Investor.gov professional search tool](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional). To learn more about navigating these filings, review our guide on [how to read Form ADV](/guides/how-to-read-form-adv/) and our overview of [what is a registered investment advisor](/guides/what-is-a-registered-investment-advisor/)."
+      },
+      {
+        "heading": "Situations Where Hiring an Adviser Does Not Fit",
+        "body": "Hiring an investment manager is not necessary for every investor. Hiring an adviser does not fit if you want self-direction and accept responsibility for every decision. Investor.gov notes that ways to invest on your own include online investing, direct investing, and dividend reinvestment plans.\n\n An asset-based fee can represent a larger share of a small account balance. \n\nYour approach changes if you only need infrequent trade execution rather than ongoing portfolio supervision. A registered broker paid per transaction offers a different model that avoids ongoing asset-based management fees. Investor.gov notes that how financial professionals make money creates specific conflicts of interest, so you must always verify fee structures before entering into an advisory agreement."
+      },
+      {
+        "heading": "Our Takeaway on What Is Investment Management",
+        "body": "Our takeaway is to invest on your own if you can create a financial plan, manage risk tolerance, and accept complete responsibility for your investment decisions. Conversely, hire an investment adviser if you need ongoing portfolio monitoring, desire discretionary trade execution, and accept an asset-based fee calculated on your assets under management. This choice applies to investors who hold investable assets and must balance continuous oversight against ongoing advisory fees.\n\nThis decision changes if your investable balance is small relative to total advisory expenses, or if your needs are met through single transactions executed by a broker. To determine your next step regarding what is investment management, write down your timeline and risk goals, review our guide on [what a financial advisor does](/guides/what-does-a-financial-advisor-do/), and compare a prospective adviser's Form CRS against investing on your own."
+      }
+    ],
+    "tools": [
+      {
+        "href": "/investing/",
+        "label": "Investing"
