       {
         "heading": "Who a Robo-Advisor Does Not Fit",
-        "body": "Our guide on [whether a financial advisor is worth it](/guides/financial-advisor-worth-it/) compares a robo-advisor with a human advisor.\n\nThe SEC bulletin also notes that in some cases robo-advisers may not have been tested under stressed market conditions. If you want a person to talk to during a downturn, a robo-advisor that offers only technical support leaves you without one.\n\n| Your situation | Robo-advisor fit | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Comfortable with a questionnaire, online disclosures and limited human contact | Fits | SEC bulletin |\n| Want a person to talk to during downturns | Fits only with hybrid access | SEC bulletin |\n| Debts, real estate or retirement accounts the questionnaire skips | The advice leaves them out | SEC bulletin |\n| Want lower fees and lower minimums | Often fits. Add up total costs first | Investor.gov glossary, SEC bulletin |\n\nOur answer changes in two cases. If a robo-advisor's questionnaire asks about your debts, real estate and outside retirement accounts, the information objection shrinks. If it offers a hybrid human adviser, or a person for accounts above a minimum size you meet, the downturn objection shrinks. Our [best robo-advisors roundup](/roundup/best-robo-advisors/) ranks individual providers."
